WEBVTT

00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:02.164
Last video ended on this curve,

00:00:02.705 --> 00:00:05.291
y equals sec x, which is one over

00:00:05.331 --> 00:00:05.832
cos x.

00:00:06.615 --> 00:00:09.044
At x equals zero it sits at one,

00:00:09.407 --> 00:00:10.189
at the bottom of a U.

00:00:11.196 --> 00:00:13.231
Picture a tower one unit tall in the

00:00:13.293 --> 00:00:13.453
sun.

00:00:14.236 --> 00:00:16.522
When the sun is x radians from straight

00:00:16.582 --> 00:00:19.387
overhead, the shadow is tan x long,

00:00:19.909 --> 00:00:21.713
and the sunbeam from the roof's edge to

00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:23.958
the shadow's tip is sec x long.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.845
With the sun straight overhead, that beam is

00:00:26.946 --> 00:00:28.288
shortest, just one.

00:00:29.271 --> 00:00:31.037
Here's a tempting guess for the derivative of

00:00:31.159 --> 00:00:31.700
sec x.

00:00:32.343 --> 00:00:35.611
Cos x has derivative minus sine x, so

00:00:35.793 --> 00:00:38.600
maybe one over cos x has derivative one

00:00:38.721 --> 00:00:39.843
over minus sine x.

00:00:40.285 --> 00:00:40.788
Just flip it.

00:00:41.716 --> 00:00:44.146
At x equals zero, that's one over zero,

00:00:44.408 --> 00:00:45.472
which doesn't even exist.

00:00:46.076 --> 00:00:48.064
But the tangent there is plainly flat.

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:50.456
So flipping the derivative fails.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.474
How do sec, cosec and cot really change

00:00:54.515 --> 00:00:55.277
when x changes?

00:00:56.075 --> 00:00:58.380
Let's watch sec x next to the curve

00:00:58.421 --> 00:01:00.065
it comes from, cos x.

00:01:00.848 --> 00:01:03.275
At zero, cos x is flat at its

00:01:03.336 --> 00:01:05.863
peak, so one over cos x is flat

00:01:06.024 --> 00:01:07.287
at the bottom of its U.

00:01:08.254 --> 00:01:10.408
Now move x towards pi over two.

00:01:10.952 --> 00:01:13.678
Cos x falls towards zero, so one over

00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:14.520
it rises.

00:01:15.203 --> 00:01:18.189
And it rises faster and faster, because one

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.952
over a tiny number is huge.

00:01:20.795 --> 00:01:23.098
At pi over four, cos x has slope

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:26.003
about minus point seven one, and sec x

00:01:26.064 --> 00:01:28.467
has slope about plus one point four one.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.581
Nearer the wall, cos x is small, and

00:01:31.663 --> 00:01:32.747
sec x is steep.

00:01:33.471 --> 00:01:36.381
So the reciprocal moves the opposite way, and

00:01:36.542 --> 00:01:39.472
as cos x shrinks, sec x gets steeper.

00:01:40.062 --> 00:01:42.557
The quotient rule video already gave us this.

00:01:43.341 --> 00:01:46.348
One over g has derivative minus g prime,

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:47.731
over g squared.

00:01:48.494 --> 00:01:49.960
The minus is the opposite way.

00:01:50.704 --> 00:01:52.708
The g squared on the bottom is why

00:01:52.729 --> 00:01:54.854
a small g makes a steep curve.

00:01:55.676 --> 00:01:58.441
For sec x, g is cos x, and

00:01:58.502 --> 00:02:00.686
g prime is minus sine x.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:03.978
So the derivative is sine x over cos

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.589
squared x, wherever cos x isn't zero.

00:02:07.453 --> 00:02:10.038
Split that into one over cos x, times

00:02:10.239 --> 00:02:11.501
sine x over cos x.

00:02:12.346 --> 00:02:13.780
That's sec x tan x.

00:02:14.566 --> 00:02:16.736
At zero, tan is zero, so the slope

00:02:16.837 --> 00:02:18.444
is zero, the flat bottom we saw.

00:02:19.327 --> 00:02:21.609
At pi over four, sec x is root

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.311
two and tan x is one,

00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:26.356
so the slope is root two, about one

00:02:26.417 --> 00:02:28.639
point four one, just as the graph showed.

00:02:29.288 --> 00:02:31.972
Cosec x is one over sine x, so

00:02:31.993 --> 00:02:34.516
the same rule works with g equals sine

00:02:34.537 --> 00:02:34.657
x.

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:37.046
Now g prime is cos x.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.355
So the derivative is minus cos x, over

00:02:40.436 --> 00:02:43.221
sine squared x, wherever sine x isn't zero.

00:02:44.124 --> 00:02:45.027
Split it the same way:

00:02:45.529 --> 00:02:48.299
minus one over sine x, times cos x

00:02:48.461 --> 00:02:49.062
over sine x.

00:02:49.706 --> 00:02:51.960
That's minus cosec x cot x.

00:02:52.927 --> 00:02:54.880
Look at the graph between zero and pi

00:02:54.921 --> 00:02:55.303
over two.

00:02:56.008 --> 00:02:59.241
Sine x rises there, so cosec x falls,

00:02:59.704 --> 00:03:00.887
and that's its minus sign.

00:03:01.592 --> 00:03:03.905
At pi over four, the slope is minus

00:03:03.946 --> 00:03:04.407
root two.

00:03:05.373 --> 00:03:08.520
Past pi over two, sine falls, so cosec

00:03:08.741 --> 00:03:09.362
rises again.

00:03:10.225 --> 00:03:12.591
In between, at pi over two, both are

00:03:12.693 --> 00:03:15.019
flat, and cot of pi over two is

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.045
zero, so the formula agrees.

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:19.892
Cot x is cos x over sine x,

00:03:20.254 --> 00:03:21.777
which is one over tan x.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.325
It's a quotient, so use the quotient rule,

00:03:24.587 --> 00:03:25.449
just as we did for tan.

00:03:26.432 --> 00:03:29.177
The top's derivative is minus sine x, and

00:03:29.218 --> 00:03:30.400
the bottom's is cos x.

00:03:31.102 --> 00:03:33.148
So the top of the answer is minus

00:03:33.229 --> 00:03:36.338
sine x times sine x, minus cos x

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:37.321
times cos x.

00:03:38.124 --> 00:03:40.248
Take out the minus, and what's left is

00:03:40.309 --> 00:03:42.934
sine squared plus cos squared, which is one.

00:03:43.817 --> 00:03:46.259
So the derivative of cot x is minus

00:03:46.421 --> 00:03:49.043
one over sine squared x, which is minus

00:03:49.144 --> 00:03:52.107
cosec squared x, again wherever sine x isn't

00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:52.468
zero.

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:55.729
That's tan's proof with the opposite sign.

00:03:56.373 --> 00:03:58.159
Tan's slope is never below one.

00:03:58.682 --> 00:04:00.686
Cot's is never above minus one,

00:04:01.189 --> 00:04:03.072
so cot decreases on each branch.

00:04:03.955 --> 00:04:06.758
At pi over four, cosec x is root

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.383
two, so the slope is minus root two

00:04:09.484 --> 00:04:11.085
squared, which is minus two.

00:04:11.862 --> 00:04:12.727
Notice the domains.

00:04:13.472 --> 00:04:16.628
Sec x, like tan x, breaks where cos

00:04:16.688 --> 00:04:17.512
x is zero.

00:04:18.055 --> 00:04:20.805
Cosec x and cot x break where sine

00:04:20.865 --> 00:04:23.234
x is zero, at every whole number of

00:04:23.274 --> 00:04:23.615
pis.

00:04:24.458 --> 00:04:26.780
On the graphs, sec x shares its walls

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.482
with tan x, and cosec x shares its

00:04:29.522 --> 00:04:30.622
walls with cot x,

00:04:31.263 --> 00:04:33.444
at the places where sine x crosses the

00:04:33.524 --> 00:04:33.805
axis.

00:04:34.706 --> 00:04:37.411
Each derivative lives exactly where its function does,

00:04:37.934 --> 00:04:39.877
so write the condition with the answer,

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:40.959
every time.

00:04:41.528 --> 00:04:42.735
Now a worked example.

00:04:43.278 --> 00:04:46.021
Find where the curve y equals sec x

00:04:46.363 --> 00:04:49.386
has a horizontal tangent, for x between minus

00:04:49.427 --> 00:04:51.950
pi over two and three pi over two.

00:04:52.857 --> 00:04:53.485
Domain first.

00:04:54.053 --> 00:04:56.727
Cos x can't be zero, so x isn't

00:04:56.788 --> 00:04:57.491
pi over two.

00:04:58.336 --> 00:05:01.044
A horizontal tangent means the derivative is zero,

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:04.115
so sec x tan x equals zero.

00:05:04.919 --> 00:05:06.943
Sec x is one over cos x, and

00:05:07.245 --> 00:05:09.028
one over a number is never zero.

00:05:09.511 --> 00:05:11.245
So tan x must be zero.

00:05:11.912 --> 00:05:14.444
In this interval, that's x equals zero and

00:05:14.606 --> 00:05:15.510
x equals pi.

00:05:16.193 --> 00:05:19.678
The points are zero, one, and pi, minus

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.842
one: the bottom of one U, and the

00:05:21.903 --> 00:05:23.745
top of the next, upside-down U.

00:05:24.568 --> 00:05:26.736
Here's a pattern that halves what you memorise,

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:29.026
now you know where the results come from.

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:32.658
Pair each function with its co-partner: sine and

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:35.628
cos, tan and cot, sec and cosec.

00:05:36.331 --> 00:05:38.136
Swap every name in a derivative for its

00:05:38.177 --> 00:05:39.680
partner, and add a minus.

00:05:40.444 --> 00:05:42.732
Sine to cos becomes cos to minus sine.

00:05:43.335 --> 00:05:45.640
Tan to sec squared becomes cot to minus

00:05:45.721 --> 00:05:46.502
cosec squared.

00:05:47.306 --> 00:05:49.979
Sec to sec tan becomes cosec to minus

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:50.602
cosec cot.

00:05:51.608 --> 00:05:54.474
It works because each co-function is its partner

00:05:54.595 --> 00:05:56.439
of pi over two minus x,

00:05:57.122 --> 00:05:59.508
and that inside has derivative minus one.

00:06:00.573 --> 00:06:02.242
On the tower, measure the sun from the

00:06:02.323 --> 00:06:03.368
horizon instead.

00:06:04.072 --> 00:06:06.275
That angle is pi over two minus x,

00:06:06.697 --> 00:06:08.519
so the same shadow is cot of it,

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:10.482
and the beam is cosec of it.

00:06:11.245 --> 00:06:13.632
As one angle grows, the other shrinks just

00:06:13.693 --> 00:06:14.134
as fast.

00:06:14.837 --> 00:06:16.724
That's why the co-functions carry the minus.

00:06:19.503 --> 00:06:19.968
Your turn.

00:06:20.712 --> 00:06:23.521
Find the exact gradient of the curve y

00:06:23.722 --> 00:06:26.590
equals cosec x, at the point where x

00:06:26.751 --> 00:06:27.934
equals pi over six.

00:06:28.741 --> 00:06:29.574
Pause here to answer.

00:06:40.276 --> 00:06:42.747
d y by d x is minus cosec

00:06:42.989 --> 00:06:43.812
x cot x.

00:06:44.637 --> 00:06:46.521
At pi over six, sine is a half,

00:06:46.883 --> 00:06:49.268
so cosec x is two, and cot x

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:50.070
is root three.

00:06:50.714 --> 00:06:53.072
So the gradient is minus two root three.

00:06:53.757 --> 00:06:56.306
Between zero and pi over two cosec x

00:06:56.408 --> 00:06:58.656
is falling, so a negative slope makes sense.

00:06:59.319 --> 00:07:01.063
Two over root three comes from flipping the

00:07:01.105 --> 00:07:03.471
derivative of sine, which is the hook's mistake.

00:07:05.423 --> 00:07:05.828
Last one.

00:07:06.472 --> 00:07:10.840
Differentiate y equals cosec x plus cot x,

00:07:11.421 --> 00:07:13.444
and state the values of x for which

00:07:13.504 --> 00:07:14.807
the derivative exists.

00:07:15.411 --> 00:07:16.237
Pause here to answer.

00:07:27.048 --> 00:07:29.833
Both terms break where sine x is zero,

00:07:30.175 --> 00:07:31.878
so x can't be any whole number of

00:07:31.979 --> 00:07:32.359
pis.

00:07:33.161 --> 00:07:36.027
Cosec x gives minus cosec x cot x,

00:07:36.469 --> 00:07:39.274
and cot x gives minus cosec squared x.

00:07:39.816 --> 00:07:42.000
So d y by d x is minus

00:07:42.061 --> 00:07:45.186
cosec x cot x, minus cosec squared x,

00:07:45.628 --> 00:07:47.251
for x not equal to k pi.

00:07:48.074 --> 00:07:50.318
A plus on the cosec squared term gives

00:07:50.399 --> 00:07:52.743
cot the slope sign of tan, but cot

00:07:52.945 --> 00:07:53.605
always falls.

00:07:55.301 --> 00:07:58.107
So each reciprocal derivative comes from one over

00:07:58.187 --> 00:08:00.673
g, or from the quotient rule, and keeps

00:08:00.733 --> 00:08:01.796
its function's domain.

00:08:02.758 --> 00:08:04.521
Next, the composites we've left for later:

00:08:05.142 --> 00:08:07.026
three to the two x, a log with

00:08:07.066 --> 00:08:09.331
base three, and tan of e to the

00:08:09.491 --> 00:08:09.611
x.

00:08:10.574 --> 00:08:12.984
Here's y equals three to the two x.

00:08:13.686 --> 00:08:14.910
What's its slope at zero,

00:08:15.411 --> 00:08:17.496
and which factors must the derivative keep?

00:08:21.201 --> 00:08:22.004
Here are the key notes.

00:08:22.647 --> 00:08:24.375
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:24.436 --> 00:08:24.918
to copy them.
